1
00:04:32,856 --> 00:04:35,609
<i>Next subject: Kowalski, Leon.</i>

2
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:37,986
<i>Engineer, waste disposal.</i>

3
00:04:38,195 --> 00:04:41,072
<i>File section: New employee, six days.</i>

4
00:04:42,449 --> 00:04:43,867
<i>Calling Mr. Webber...</i>

5
00:04:44,034 --> 00:04:46,870
- Come in.
<i>- ...please report to Zone A Sector 9.</i>

6
00:04:50,415 --> 00:04:54,836
<i>Replication Center Level 9,
we have a B1 security alert.</i>

7
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:57,589
<i>Stand by for ID check, please.</i>

8
00:04:58,215 --> 00:04:59,257
Sit down.

9
00:04:59,883 --> 00:05:03,053
<i>Replication Center Level 9,
we have a B1 security alert.</i>

10
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:04,262
<i>Stand by for...</i>

11
00:05:04,429 --> 00:05:05,722
Care if I talk?

12
00:05:06,223 --> 00:05:08,058
I'm kind of nervous when I take tests.

13
00:05:08,225 --> 00:05:10,060
Just please don't move.

14
00:05:11,228 --> 00:05:12,896
Oh, sorry.

15
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:19,236
I already had an IQ test.
I don't think I've had one of these.

16
00:05:19,402 --> 00:05:21,655
Reaction time is a factor,
so pay attention.

17
00:05:21,822 --> 00:05:24,407
Now, answer as quickly as you can.

18
00:05:24,908 --> 00:05:26,409
Sure.

19
00:05:27,410 --> 00:05:29,079
1187 at Hunterwasser.

20
00:05:29,246 --> 00:05:30,247
That's the hotel.

21
00:05:30,413 --> 00:05:32,582
- What?
- Where I live.

22
00:05:32,749 --> 00:05:34,417
- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.

23
00:05:34,584 --> 00:05:36,253
Is that part of the test?

24
00:05:36,419 --> 00:05:39,089
No. Just warming you up. That's all.

25
00:05:40,048 --> 00:05:42,300
It's not fancy or anything.

26
00:05:43,426 --> 00:05:46,012
You're in a desert,
walking along in the sand, when--

27
00:05:46,179 --> 00:05:47,264
Is this the test now?

28
00:05:47,430 --> 00:05:49,933
Yes. You're in a desert,
walking along in the sand...

29
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:51,810
- ...when you look down--
- What one?

30
00:05:51,977 --> 00:05:53,728
- What?
- What desert?

31
00:05:53,895 --> 00:05:56,690
Doesn't make any difference.
It's completely hypothetical.

32
00:05:56,857 --> 00:05:59,234
- But how come I'd be there?
- Maybe you're fed up.

33
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:01,695
Maybe you wanna be by yourself.
Who knows?

34
00:06:02,362 --> 00:06:03,989
You look down and see a tortoise.

35
00:06:04,155 --> 00:06:07,784
- It's crawling towards you--
- Tortoise? What's that?

36
00:06:10,871 --> 00:06:13,290
- You know what a turtle is?
- Of course.

37
00:06:13,456 --> 00:06:15,041
Same thing.

38
00:06:15,208 --> 00:06:16,793
I've never seen a turtle.

39
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:20,964
But I understand what you mean.

40
00:06:21,131 --> 00:06:23,800
You reach down,
you flip the tortoise over on its back.

41
00:06:23,967 --> 00:06:26,344
Do you make up these questions,
Mr. Holden?

42
00:06:26,511 --> 00:06:28,638
Or do they write them down for you?

43
00:06:28,805 --> 00:06:31,766
The tortoise lays on its back,
its belly baking in the sun...

44
00:06:31,933 --> 00:06:34,978
...beating its legs,
trying to turn itself over, but it can't...

45
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:36,646
...not without your help.

46
00:06:36,813 --> 00:06:39,107
- You're not helping.
- What do you mean I'm not?

47
00:06:39,274 --> 00:06:40,609
I mean, you're not helping.

48
00:06:41,151 --> 00:06:42,235
Why is that, Leon?

49
00:06:50,827 --> 00:06:53,330
They're just questions, Leon.

50
00:06:53,997 --> 00:06:56,875
In answer to your query,
they're written down for me.

51
00:06:57,042 --> 00:07:01,338
It's a test designed to provoke
an emotional response.

52
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:05,884
Shall we continue?

53
00:07:07,052 --> 00:07:10,847
Describe in single words only the
good things that come into your mind...

54
00:07:11,556 --> 00:07:13,224
...about your mother.

55
00:07:13,391 --> 00:07:15,936
- My mother?
- Yeah.

56
00:07:17,187 --> 00:07:19,689
Let me tell you about my mother.

57
00:07:35,538 --> 00:07:39,542
<i>A new life awaits you
in the Off-world colonies.</i>

58
00:07:39,876 --> 00:07:41,753
<i>The chance to begin again...</i>

59
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:45,966
<i>...in a golden land of opportunity
and adventure.</i>

60
00:08:11,866 --> 00:08:16,079
<i>A new life awaits you
in the Off-world colonies.</i>

61
00:08:16,287 --> 00:08:18,248
<i>The chance to begin again...</i>

62
00:08:18,415 --> 00:08:22,043
<i>...in a golden land of opportunity
and adventure.</i>

63
00:08:25,422 --> 00:08:27,090
<i>The custom-tailored...</i>

64
00:08:27,257 --> 00:08:29,509
<i>...genetically engineered
humanoid Replicant...</i>

65
00:08:29,676 --> 00:08:32,721
<i>...designed especially for your needs.</i>

66
00:08:32,929 --> 00:08:36,725
<i>So come on, America.
Let's put out...</i>

67
00:08:38,351 --> 00:08:40,061
Give me four.

68
00:08:42,063 --> 00:08:43,857
No, four. Two, two. Four.

69
00:08:47,569 --> 00:08:48,820
And noodles.

70
00:09:02,459 --> 00:09:03,668
Hey.

71
00:09:12,093 --> 00:09:14,679
He say you under arrest, Mr. Deckard.

72
00:09:15,597 --> 00:09:17,057
Got the wrong guy, pal.

73
00:09:22,687 --> 00:09:24,105
He say you Blade Runner.

74
00:09:24,272 --> 00:09:25,732
Tell him I'm eating.

75
00:09:25,899 --> 00:09:27,108
Captain Bryant...

76
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:32,363
Bryant, huh?

77
00:09:47,712 --> 00:09:49,798
<i>Yellow 3.</i>

78
00:09:50,006 --> 00:09:52,634
<i>Climb and maintain 4000.</i>

79
00:10:55,071 --> 00:10:57,157
<i>Final descent.</i>

80
00:10:57,323 --> 00:11:00,285
<i>Now on glide path. Mark course.</i>

81
00:11:00,451 --> 00:11:02,495
<i>Over the landing threshold.</i>

82
00:11:21,181 --> 00:11:22,223
Hi, Deck.

83
00:11:22,390 --> 00:11:23,683
Bryant.

84
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:27,145
You wouldn't have come
if I just asked you to. Sit down, pal.

85
00:11:28,605 --> 00:11:32,942
Come on, don't be an asshole, Deckard.
I've got four skin-jobs walking the streets.

86
00:11:49,250 --> 00:11:52,337
They jumped a shuttle Off-world,
killed the crew and passengers.

87
00:11:52,503 --> 00:11:55,548
They found the shuttle drifting
off the coast two weeks ago...

88
00:11:55,715 --> 00:11:57,675
...so we know they're around.

89
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:00,220
Embarrassing.

90
00:12:00,386 --> 00:12:03,848
No, not embarrassing. No one's ever
gonna find out they're down here.

91
00:12:04,015 --> 00:12:06,601
Because you're gonna spot them
and air them out.

92
00:12:06,768 --> 00:12:08,061
I don't work here anymore.

93
00:12:09,437 --> 00:12:10,521
Give it to Holden.

94
00:12:11,022 --> 00:12:12,982
- He's good.
- I did.

95
00:12:13,149 --> 00:12:15,693
He can breathe okay
as long as nobody unplugs him.

96
00:12:16,527 --> 00:12:19,739
He's not good enough.
Not good as you.

97
00:12:19,906 --> 00:12:21,532
I need you, Deck.

98
00:12:21,699 --> 00:12:24,035
Now, this is a bad one, the worst yet.

99
00:12:24,869 --> 00:12:26,788
I need the old Blade Runner.

100
00:12:26,955 --> 00:12:28,539
I need your magic.

101
00:12:29,874 --> 00:12:32,752
I was quit
when I come in here, Bryant.

102
00:12:33,544 --> 00:12:35,463
I'm twice as quit now.

103
00:12:35,672 --> 00:12:38,549
Stop right where you are.
You know the score, pal?

104
00:12:39,217 --> 00:12:41,469
If you're not cop, you're little people.

105
00:12:53,564 --> 00:12:54,983
No choice, huh?

106
00:12:56,484 --> 00:12:58,486
No choice, pal.

107
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:01,906
<i>I already had an IQ test this year.</i>

108
00:13:02,073 --> 00:13:04,033
<i>I don't think I've ever
had one of these.</i>

109
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:06,786
<i>Reaction time is a factor,
so please pay attention.</i>

110
00:13:06,953 --> 00:13:09,080
<i>- Answer as quickly as you can.
- Yeah, sure.</i>

111
00:13:09,247 --> 00:13:12,250
<i>1187 at Hunterwasser.</i>

112
00:13:12,417 --> 00:13:13,793
<i>Yeah. That's the hotel.</i>

113
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:16,254
<i>- What?
- Where I live.</i>

114
00:13:16,421 --> 00:13:18,631
<i>- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.</i>

115
00:13:18,798 --> 00:13:21,801
There was an escape from
the Off-world colonies two weeks ago.

116
00:13:21,968 --> 00:13:24,429
Six Replicants:
three male, three female.

117
00:13:24,595 --> 00:13:27,098
They slaughtered 23 people
and jumped a shuttle.

118
00:13:27,265 --> 00:13:29,600
An aerial patrol spotted
the ship off the coast.

119
00:13:29,767 --> 00:13:31,519
No crew, no sight of them.

120
00:13:31,728 --> 00:13:34,814
Three nights ago they tried to break
into Tyrell Corporation.

121
00:13:34,981 --> 00:13:37,358
Two of them got fried running
through an electrical field.

122
00:13:37,525 --> 00:13:39,444
We lost the others.

123
00:13:40,445 --> 00:13:44,115
On the possibility they might try
to infiltrate as employees...

124
00:13:44,282 --> 00:13:48,453
...I had Holden go over and run
Voight-Kampff tests on the new workers.

125
00:13:48,619 --> 00:13:50,413
Looks like he got himself one.

126
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:54,208
<i>So you look down and you see
a tortoise. It's crawling towards you.</i>

127
00:13:54,375 --> 00:13:55,543
<i>- Tortoise? What's that?</i>
- That's Leon.

128
00:13:55,710 --> 00:13:57,879
Ammunition loader
on intergalactic runs.

129
00:13:58,046 --> 00:14:01,132
He can lift 400-pound atomic loads
all day and night.

130
00:14:01,299 --> 00:14:03,718
The only <i>way</i> you can hurt him
is to kill him.

131
00:14:03,885 --> 00:14:08,306
I don't get it. What do they risk coming
back to Earth for? That's unusual. Why...?

132
00:14:08,473 --> 00:14:11,184
What do they want
out of the Tyrell Corporation?

133
00:14:11,351 --> 00:14:14,145
Well, you tell me, pal.
That's what you're here for.

134
00:14:25,406 --> 00:14:29,077
- What's this?
- Nexus 6. Roy Batty.

135
00:14:29,285 --> 00:14:31,204
Incept date: 2016.

136
00:14:31,371 --> 00:14:34,123
Combat model.
Optimum self-sufficiency.

137
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,418
Probably the leader.

138
00:14:40,838 --> 00:14:42,507
This is Zhora.

139
00:14:42,673 --> 00:14:45,134
She's trained for
an Off-world kick murder squad.

140
00:14:45,885 --> 00:14:49,806
Talk about Beauty and the Beast.
She's both.

141
00:14:53,851 --> 00:14:57,772
The fourth skin-job is Pris,
a basic pleasure model.

142
00:14:57,939 --> 00:15:01,359
The standard item for military clubs
in the outer colonies.

143
00:15:02,985 --> 00:15:06,781
They were designed to copy human beings
in every <i>way</i> except their emotions.

144
00:15:06,948 --> 00:15:09,033
The designers reckoned
after a few years...

145
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:11,702
...they might develop
their own emotional responses.

146
00:15:12,703 --> 00:15:15,289
Oh, hate, love, fear, anger, envy.

147
00:15:15,456 --> 00:15:17,708
So they built in a fail-safe device.

148
00:15:17,875 --> 00:15:21,212
- Which is what?
- Four-year lifespan.

149
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:30,888
Now, there's a Nexus 6
over at the Tyrell Corporation.

150
00:15:31,055 --> 00:15:35,226
- I want you to go put the machine on it.
- And if the machine doesn't work?

151
00:17:06,859 --> 00:17:07,944
Do you like our owl?

152
00:17:11,447 --> 00:17:13,157
It's artificial?

153
00:17:14,158 --> 00:17:16,118
Of course it is.

154
00:17:17,453 --> 00:17:18,788
Must be expensive.

155
00:17:20,998 --> 00:17:23,501
Very. I'm Rachael.

156
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:25,461
Deckard.

157
00:17:27,964 --> 00:17:32,009
It seems you feel our work
is not a benefit to the public.

158
00:17:32,969 --> 00:17:37,139
Replicants are like any other machine:
They're either a benefit or a hazard.

159
00:17:37,306 --> 00:17:40,059
If they're a benefit,
it's not my problem.

160
00:17:40,226 --> 00:17:43,104
May I ask you a personal question?

161
00:17:44,730 --> 00:17:46,482
Sure.

162
00:17:50,486 --> 00:17:52,822
Have you ever retired a human
by mistake?

163
00:17:55,157 --> 00:17:56,158
No.

164
00:17:56,325 --> 00:18:00,162
- But in your position, that is a risk.
- Is this to be an empathy test?

165
00:18:01,497 --> 00:18:05,042
Capillary dilation
of the so-called blush response?

166
00:18:05,668 --> 00:18:08,921
Fluctuation of the pupil.

167
00:18:09,839 --> 00:18:12,925
Involuntary dilation of the iris.

168
00:18:14,343 --> 00:18:16,304
We call it Voight-Kampff for short.

169
00:18:17,471 --> 00:18:19,515
Mr. Deckard, Dr. Eldon Tyrell.

170
00:18:20,516 --> 00:18:22,435
Demonstrate it.

171
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:24,604
I wanna see it work.

172
00:18:24,770 --> 00:18:27,398
- Where's the subject?
- I wanna see it work on a person.

173
00:18:27,565 --> 00:18:30,526
I wanna see a negative
before I provide you with a positive.

174
00:18:30,693 --> 00:18:32,361
What's that gonna prove?

175
00:18:33,404 --> 00:18:35,364
Indulge me.

176
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:37,033
On you?

177
00:18:37,199 --> 00:18:38,534
Try her.

178
00:18:43,914 --> 00:18:46,167
It's too bright in here.

179
00:19:23,496 --> 00:19:25,539
Do you mind if I smoke?

180
00:19:26,207 --> 00:19:27,583
It won't affect the test.

181
00:19:29,752 --> 00:19:32,922
All right, I'm gonna ask you
a series of questions.

182
00:19:33,089 --> 00:19:36,509
Just relax and answer them
as simply as you can.

183
00:19:44,016 --> 00:19:47,269
It's your birthday.
Someone gives you a calfskin wallet.

184
00:19:47,436 --> 00:19:49,230
I wouldn't accept it.

185
00:19:49,397 --> 00:19:53,818
Also, I'd report the person
who gave it to me to the police.

186
00:19:54,944 --> 00:19:58,280
You've got a little boy.
He shows you his butterfly collection...

187
00:19:58,447 --> 00:20:00,282
...plus the killing jar.

188
00:20:03,786 --> 00:20:05,287
I'd take him to the doctor.

189
00:20:11,293 --> 00:20:12,670
You're watching television.

190
00:20:12,837 --> 00:20:15,548
Suddenly you realize
there's a wasp crawling on your arm.

191
00:20:15,756 --> 00:20:17,967
I'd kill it.

192
00:20:20,803 --> 00:20:24,473
You're reading a magazine. You come
across a full-page nude photo of a girl.

193
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:27,810
Is this testing whether I'm a Replicant
or a lesbian, Mr. Deckard?

194
00:20:27,977 --> 00:20:30,312
Just answer the questions, please.

195
00:20:34,775 --> 00:20:36,360
You show it to your husband.

196
00:20:36,527 --> 00:20:39,155
He likes it so much
he hangs it on your bedroom wall.

197
00:20:39,321 --> 00:20:40,656
--Bush outside your window?

198
00:20:40,823 --> 00:20:42,032
I wouldn't let him.

199
00:20:42,199 --> 00:20:43,868
orange body, green legs?

200
00:20:44,034 --> 00:20:45,327
Why not?

201
00:20:45,494 --> 00:20:47,663
I should be enough for him.

202
00:20:51,375 --> 00:20:53,377
One more question.

203
00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:57,214
You're watching a stage play,
a banquet is in progress.

204
00:20:57,381 --> 00:21:01,510
The guests are enjoying
an appetizer of raw oysters.

205
00:21:01,677 --> 00:21:04,805
The entrée consists of boiled dog.

206
00:21:21,363 --> 00:21:23,949
Would you step out
for a few moments, Rachael?

207
00:21:33,584 --> 00:21:35,252
Thank you.

208
00:21:36,504 --> 00:21:38,881
She's a Replicant, isn't she?

209
00:21:39,048 --> 00:21:40,800
I'm impressed.

210
00:21:40,966 --> 00:21:43,636
How many questions
does it usually take to spot one?

211
00:21:43,844 --> 00:21:46,555
- I don't get it, Tyrell.
- How many questions?

212
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:49,391
Twenty, thirty, cross-referenced.

213
00:21:49,642 --> 00:21:52,102
It took more than a hundred for Rachael,
didn't it?

214
00:21:52,269 --> 00:21:54,104
She doesn't know.

215
00:21:54,271 --> 00:21:56,065
She's beginning to suspect, I think.

216
00:21:56,232 --> 00:21:58,317
Suspect?
How can it not know what it is?

217
00:21:58,859 --> 00:22:01,654
Commerce is our goal here at Tyrell.

218
00:22:01,862 --> 00:22:04,073
"More human than human"
is our motto.

219
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:07,910
Rachael is an experiment,
nothing more.

220
00:22:08,077 --> 00:22:12,498
We began to recognize in them
a strange obsession.

221
00:22:12,665 --> 00:22:16,085
After all, they are emotionally
inexperienced, with only a few years...

222
00:22:16,252 --> 00:22:19,755
...in which to store up the experiences
which you and I take for granted.

223
00:22:19,922 --> 00:22:25,427
If we gift them with a past, we create
a cushion or a pillow for their emotions...

224
00:22:25,594 --> 00:22:28,430
...then consequently
we can control them better.

225
00:22:28,597 --> 00:22:30,432
Memories.

226
00:22:30,599 --> 00:22:32,518
You're talking about memories.

227
00:22:42,778 --> 00:22:45,322
HOLDEN <i>Reaction
time is a factor, so please pay attention.</i>

228
00:22:45,489 --> 00:22:47,449
<i>- Now, answer as quickly as you can.</i>
<i>- Sure.</i>

229
00:22:47,616 --> 00:22:50,286
<i>- 1187 at Hunterwasser.
- Yeah. That's the hotel.</i>

230
00:22:50,452 --> 00:22:51,620
<i>- What?
- Where I live.</i>

231
00:22:51,787 --> 00:22:53,455
<i>- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.</i>

232
00:22:53,622 --> 00:22:55,457
<i>- Is that part of the test?
- No.</i>

233
00:23:25,988 --> 00:23:28,115
Kowalski.

234
00:25:15,264 --> 00:25:17,641
Time...

235
00:25:17,808 --> 00:25:19,059
...enough.

236
00:25:37,119 --> 00:25:40,080
Did you get your precious photos?

237
00:25:42,875 --> 00:25:45,085
Someone was there.

238
00:25:45,669 --> 00:25:47,463
Men?

239
00:25:51,133 --> 00:25:54,595
Policemen?

240
00:26:43,852 --> 00:26:45,354
Aha.

241
00:27:26,728 --> 00:27:29,940
<i>Fiery the angels fell</i>

242
00:27:30,107 --> 00:27:33,402
<i>Deep thunder rolled around their shores</i>

243
00:27:33,777 --> 00:27:37,114
<i>Burning with the fires of Orc</i>

244
00:27:39,908 --> 00:27:43,412
You not come here! Illegal!

245
00:27:46,290 --> 00:27:47,916
Hey! Hey!

246
00:27:49,251 --> 00:27:52,462
Cold! Those are my eyes! Freezing!

247
00:28:03,098 --> 00:28:04,766
Yes.

248
00:28:04,933 --> 00:28:06,435
Questions.

249
00:28:13,358 --> 00:28:15,611
Hey! Hey!

250
00:28:28,665 --> 00:28:33,212
Morphology. Longevity. Incept dates.

251
00:28:33,378 --> 00:28:34,880
Don't know.

252
00:28:35,047 --> 00:28:37,341
I don't know such stuff.

253
00:28:39,259 --> 00:28:41,178
I just do eyes.

254
00:28:41,345 --> 00:28:44,181
Just eyes, just genetic design.

255
00:28:44,348 --> 00:28:46,183
Just eyes.

256
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:50,312
You Nexus, huh?

257
00:28:50,479 --> 00:28:53,065
I design your eyes.

258
00:28:53,273 --> 00:28:55,234
Chew...

259
00:28:55,400 --> 00:29:00,489
...if only you could see
what I've seen with your eyes.

260
00:29:00,656 --> 00:29:02,574
Now...

261
00:29:02,783 --> 00:29:04,576
...questions.

262
00:29:04,785 --> 00:29:06,828
I don't know answers.

263
00:29:06,995 --> 00:29:08,830
Who does?

264
00:29:12,334 --> 00:29:13,710
Tyrell.

265
00:29:13,877 --> 00:29:15,295
He knows everything.

266
00:29:17,172 --> 00:29:19,508
Tyrell Corporation?

267
00:29:19,675 --> 00:29:23,720
He big boss. Big genius.
He design your mind.

268
00:29:23,887 --> 00:29:25,806
Your brain, huh?

269
00:29:26,932 --> 00:29:28,684
Smart.

270
00:29:30,519 --> 00:29:32,604
Very cold.

271
00:29:34,731 --> 00:29:38,360
Not an easy man to see...

272
00:29:39,194 --> 00:29:41,613
Give me coat.

273
00:29:42,823 --> 00:29:44,116
...I guess?

274
00:29:49,454 --> 00:29:51,373
Sebastian.

275
00:29:52,040 --> 00:29:55,419
He take you there.
He take you there.

276
00:29:55,585 --> 00:29:58,130
Sebastian who?

277
00:30:00,549 --> 00:30:03,427
J.F...

278
00:30:03,593 --> 00:30:06,555
...Sebast...

279
00:30:08,140 --> 00:30:09,975
Now...

280
00:30:12,436 --> 00:30:14,479
...where...

281
00:30:14,646 --> 00:30:17,733
...would we find this...

282
00:30:17,899 --> 00:30:21,361
...J.F. Sebastian?

283
00:30:21,945 --> 00:30:24,156
HOLDEN <i>Let's continue, shall we?</i>

284
00:30:24,364 --> 00:30:26,408
<i>Describe in single words...</i>

285
00:30:26,575 --> 00:30:30,245
<i>...only the good things that come
into your mind about your mother.</i>

286
00:30:30,412 --> 00:30:32,581
<i>- My mother?
- Yeah.</i>

287
00:30:32,748 --> 00:30:34,333
<i>I'll tell you about my mother.</i>

288
00:30:56,396 --> 00:30:59,483
<i>Voice-print identification.
Your floor number, please.</i>

289
00:30:59,649 --> 00:31:03,695
- Deckard, 97.
<i>- Ninety-seven. Thank you.</i> Danke.

290
00:31:24,716 --> 00:31:26,968
I wanted to see you.

291
00:31:32,349 --> 00:31:34,059
So I waited.

292
00:31:34,226 --> 00:31:36,228
Let me help you.

293
00:31:39,314 --> 00:31:41,566
What do I need help for?

294
00:31:43,443 --> 00:31:45,737
I don't know <i>why</i>
he told you what he did.

295
00:31:45,946 --> 00:31:46,988
Talk to him.

296
00:31:47,572 --> 00:31:49,825
He wouldn't see me.

297
00:32:02,087 --> 00:32:04,005
You want a drink?

298
00:32:06,675 --> 00:32:08,093
No?

299
00:32:14,266 --> 00:32:16,852
You think I'm a Replicant, don't you?

300
00:32:22,566 --> 00:32:24,192
Look.

301
00:32:24,985 --> 00:32:27,696
It's me with my mother.

302
00:32:28,697 --> 00:32:30,365
Yeah?

303
00:32:36,496 --> 00:32:39,124
Remember when you were six?

304
00:32:39,291 --> 00:32:42,961
You and your brother snuck into an empty
building through a basement window.

305
00:32:43,128 --> 00:32:45,505
You were gonna play doctor?

306
00:32:46,381 --> 00:32:49,968
He showed you his, and when it got
to be your turn, you chickened and ran.

307
00:32:50,135 --> 00:32:51,803
Remember that?

308
00:32:54,055 --> 00:32:58,393
Did you ever tell anybody that?
Your mother, Tyrell, anybody, huh?

309
00:33:00,562 --> 00:33:03,815
You remember the spider that lived
in the bush outside your window?

310
00:33:04,566 --> 00:33:06,943
Orange body, green legs?

311
00:33:07,110 --> 00:33:09,905
Watched her build a web all summer?

312
00:33:10,071 --> 00:33:12,449
Then one day, there's a big egg in it.

313
00:33:12,616 --> 00:33:14,326
The egg hatched...

314
00:33:14,534 --> 00:33:17,078
- The egg hatched...
- And?

315
00:33:17,454 --> 00:33:20,415
...and a hundred baby spiders
came out.

316
00:33:21,625 --> 00:33:22,959
And they ate her.

317
00:33:27,756 --> 00:33:29,591
Implants.

318
00:33:29,758 --> 00:33:32,469
Those aren't your memories,
they're somebody else's.

319
00:33:32,636 --> 00:33:35,096
They're Tyrell's niece's.

320
00:33:43,104 --> 00:33:44,773
Okay.

321
00:33:44,940 --> 00:33:46,525
Bad joke.

322
00:33:46,691 --> 00:33:48,276
I made a bad joke.

323
00:33:48,443 --> 00:33:50,111
You're not a Replicant.

324
00:33:50,278 --> 00:33:51,321
Go home.

325
00:33:51,488 --> 00:33:53,156
Okay?

326
00:33:55,325 --> 00:33:58,453
No, really. I'm sorry.

327
00:33:58,954 --> 00:34:00,705
Go home.

328
00:34:13,718 --> 00:34:14,803
Want a drink?

329
00:34:16,721 --> 00:34:18,974
I'll get you a drink.

330
00:34:19,140 --> 00:34:21,476
I'll get a glass.

331
00:38:05,533 --> 00:38:07,035
Hey!

332
00:38:12,207 --> 00:38:14,417
You forgot your bag.

333
00:38:24,928 --> 00:38:26,888
I'm lost.

334
00:38:28,264 --> 00:38:30,225
Don't worry, I won't hurt you.

335
00:38:34,395 --> 00:38:35,605
What's your name?

336
00:38:35,772 --> 00:38:39,108
- Pris.
- Mine's J.F. Sebastian.

337
00:38:39,275 --> 00:38:41,361
- Hi.
- Hi.

338
00:38:44,656 --> 00:38:46,741
Oh, where were you going?

339
00:38:48,785 --> 00:38:50,370
Home?

340
00:38:51,663 --> 00:38:53,581
I don't have one.

341
00:38:57,001 --> 00:38:59,254
We scared each other pretty good,
didn't we?

342
00:38:59,420 --> 00:39:01,172
We sure did.

343
00:39:06,678 --> 00:39:08,972
I'm hungry, J.F.

344
00:39:09,430 --> 00:39:11,808
I got stuff inside.

345
00:39:12,267 --> 00:39:13,685
You wanna come in?

346
00:39:13,893 --> 00:39:16,187
I was hoping you'd say that.

347
00:39:50,346 --> 00:39:52,515
Do you live in this building
all by yourself?

348
00:39:52,682 --> 00:39:55,852
Yeah, I live here
pretty much alone right now.

349
00:39:56,436 --> 00:39:59,022
No housing shortage around here.

350
00:40:01,065 --> 00:40:03,651
Plenty of room for everybody.

351
00:40:12,660 --> 00:40:14,996
Watch out for the water.

352
00:40:16,873 --> 00:40:19,834
Must get lonely here, J.F.

353
00:40:21,836 --> 00:40:24,547
Not really. I make friends.

354
00:40:24,714 --> 00:40:28,092
They're toys. My friends are toys.
I make them.

355
00:40:28,259 --> 00:40:30,595
It's a hobby. I'm a genetic designer.

356
00:40:30,762 --> 00:40:32,680
Do you know what that is?

357
00:40:32,847 --> 00:40:34,390
No.

358
00:40:36,351 --> 00:40:37,852
Now.

359
00:40:39,520 --> 00:40:41,189
Oh.

360
00:40:42,523 --> 00:40:45,360
Yoo-hoo, home again.

361
00:40:50,365 --> 00:40:53,117
Home again, home again, jiggidy-jig.

362
00:40:53,284 --> 00:40:56,371
- Good evening, J.F.
- Evening, fellas.

363
00:41:08,132 --> 00:41:10,551
They're my friends. I made them.

364
00:41:10,718 --> 00:41:12,553
Where are your folks?

365
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:14,222
I'm sort of an orphan.

366
00:41:14,389 --> 00:41:16,599
Oh. What about your friends?

367
00:41:16,766 --> 00:41:19,227
I have some, but I have to find them.

368
00:41:19,394 --> 00:41:22,021
I'll let them know
where I am tomorrow.

369
00:41:22,814 --> 00:41:24,232
Oh?

370
00:41:25,900 --> 00:41:30,238
Can I take those things for you?
They're soaked, aren't they?

371
00:43:09,253 --> 00:43:11,923
Enhance 224 to 176.

372
00:43:29,482 --> 00:43:30,942
Enhance.

373
00:43:31,109 --> 00:43:32,610
Stop.

374
00:43:46,332 --> 00:43:48,167
Move in.

375
00:43:48,334 --> 00:43:49,961
Stop.

376
00:43:50,628 --> 00:43:52,463
Pull out, track right.

377
00:43:52,630 --> 00:43:54,423
Stop.

378
